    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

    Architecture A (in-process, runtime#1063): the avatar owns the window the
    runtime weaves into, so THIS window is the one that has to be translucent —
    there is no runtime-owned overlay surface behind it any more. Three things
    have to line up or the result is an opaque black box:

      1. windowIsTranslucent      — tells WindowManager not to occlude what is
                                    behind us, so the launcher keeps drawing.
      2. windowBackground=@null   — no opaque backdrop painted under the Surface
                                    (transparent would also work; @null skips the
                                    draw entirely, which is what we want since the
                                    NativeActivity's Surface covers the window).
      3. a TRANSLUCENT pixel format on the Surface itself — set in code
         (MainActivity.onCreate → window.setFormat(PixelFormat.TRANSLUCENT)),
         because a NativeActivity's surface otherwise comes up opaque RGBX and
         SurfaceFlinger ignores our alpha no matter what the theme says.

    This is the Android analog of the Windows WS_EX_NOREDIRECTIONBITMAP + null
    brush the desktop build uses.
-->
<resources>
    <style name="Theme.Avatar.Transparent" parent="@android:style/Theme.NoTitleBar.Fullscreen">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@null</item>
        <item name="android:windowIsTranslucent">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowShowWallpaper">false</item>
        <item name="android:colorBackgroundCacheHint">@null</item>
        <item name="android:windowContentOverlay">@null</item>
        <!-- Keep the window from being treated as an opaque fullscreen layer,
             which is what lets SurfaceFlinger composite the launcher under us. -->
        <item name="android:windowDisablePreview">true</item>
    </style>
</resources>
